import{_ as a,o as n,c as s,a2 as t}from"./chunks/framework.RycU2wFZ.js";const h=JSON.parse('{"title":"Spending Policies: How to Limit Agent Spending","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"learn/02-agent-payments/spending-policy.md","filePath":"learn/02-agent-payments/spending-policy.md","lastUpdated":1792595354000}'),i={name:"learn/02-agent-payments/spending-policy.md"};function o(l,e,p,r,d,c){return n(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t(`<h1 id="spending-policies-how-to-limit-agent-spending" tabindex="-1">Spending Policies: How to Limit Agent Spending <a class="header-anchor" href="#spending-policies-how-to-limit-agent-spending" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Spending Policies: How to Limit Agent Spending&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="why-use-a-spending-policy-engine" tabindex="-1">Why use a spending policy engine? <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-use-a-spending-policy-engine" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why use a spending policy engine?&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Suppose a research agent receives a 10 USDC budget for purchasing data. It may use APIs appropriately, or repeatedly buy unnecessary data because of a model error, task loop, or malicious input. A wallet and payment API alone do not impose meaningful boundaries.</p><p>A spending policy engine applies independent rules before payment execution. It combines the role of account spending controls with gateway access controls.</p><h2 id="basic-evaluation-flow" tabindex="-1">Basic evaluation flow <a class="header-anchor" href="#basic-evaluation-flow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Basic evaluation flow&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Agent requests payment</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Authenticate agent</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Check provider and recipient</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Check per-payment amount</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Check budgets</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Check request rate</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Check risk and approval requirements</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Allow, reject, or wait for approval</span></span></code></pre></div><p>An allowed request has passed policy evaluation; funds may still need authorization, payment, fulfillment, and settlement.</p><h2 id="per-payment-limits" tabindex="-1">Per-payment limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#per-payment-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Per-payment limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A per-payment limit caps the amount of one request. For example, with <code>max_transaction_amount: 0.10</code> USDC, a 0.05 or 0.10 request passes this rule while a 0.20 request is rejected. Passing this check does not imply that other rules pass. A per-payment cap cannot control cumulative spending by itself.</p><h2 id="time-window-budgets" tabindex="-1">Time-window budgets <a class="header-anchor" href="#time-window-budgets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Time-window budgets&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A time-window budget caps cumulative spending over a defined period. A policy might set <code>daily: 2.00</code> USDC. The implementation must specify whether “daily” means a calendar day or a rolling 24-hour window, along with timezone, accounting, expiry, and concurrency rules.</p><p>With a rolling 24-hour budget, if 1.80 USDC has been used and 0.20 remains, a 0.05 request can fit while a 0.25 request cannot. Budget checks should account for in-flight reservations as well as confirmed spend.</p><h2 id="provider-allowlists" tabindex="-1">Provider allowlists <a class="header-anchor" href="#provider-allowlists" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Provider allowlists&quot;">​</a></h2><p>An allowlist limits which services may receive payment, for example <code>search-api</code>, <code>market-data</code>, and <code>translation-api</code>. An unknown provider should be rejected even for a tiny amount. Names supplied freely by a remote service are not proof of identity; the implementation must bind provider identity to trustworthy payment details.</p><h2 id="request-rate-limits" tabindex="-1">Request-rate limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#request-rate-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Request-rate limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A rule such as <code>requests_per_minute: 10</code> can limit runaway loops, duplicate traffic, and unusually frequent spending. Rate limits do not replace budgets: ten expensive calls may cost more than hundreds of cheap ones. Use rate controls together with amount and cumulative-spend limits.</p><h2 id="lifetime-limits" tabindex="-1">Lifetime limits <a class="header-anchor" href="#lifetime-limits" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Lifetime limits&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A lifetime limit caps total spending over an agent&#39;s authorization period. If an agent has a 20.00 USDC lifetime cap and has already spent 19.90, a new 0.20 request should be rejected even if the current daily budget is available. Define how refunds, failed attempts, and reservations affect this total.</p><h2 id="human-approval-thresholds" tabindex="-1">Human approval thresholds <a class="header-anchor" href="#human-approval-thresholds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Human approval thresholds&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Some requests should wait for a person. For example, a policy might require approval above 1.00 USDC. Bind approval to the exact request parameters. If the amount, recipient, or another important field changes after approval, obtain a new approval or revalidate the authorization.</p><h2 id="reserve-budget-before-proceeding" tabindex="-1">Reserve budget before proceeding <a class="header-anchor" href="#reserve-budget-before-proceeding" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reserve budget before proceeding&quot;">​</a></h2><p>If two concurrent requests each need 0.70 USDC while only 1.00 remains, checking without reserving can approve both. Instead, atomically reserve the amount before continuing:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Check available budget</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Atomically reserve amount</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Execute payment flow</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>        ↓</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>Commit spend or release reservation</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A useful accounting model distinguishes:</p><ul><li><code>available</code>: budget still free to allocate.</li><li><code>reserved</code>: budget held for in-flight requests.</li><li><code>spent</code>: confirmed consumption.</li></ul><p>In a simple model:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>available = limit - spent - reserved</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The reservation and policy decision must be concurrency-safe. Otherwise simultaneous requests can exceed the configured limit.</p><h2 id="key-takeaway" tabindex="-1">Key takeaway <a class="header-anchor" href="#key-takeaway" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Key takeaway&quot;">​</a></h2><p>No single rule is enough. Combine per-payment limits, rolling budgets, provider allowlists, rate controls, lifetime limits, and approvals as appropriate. Enforce them in deterministic code and reserve budget atomically before authorizing payment.</p>`,30)])])}const m=a(i,[["render",o]]);export{h as __pageData,m as default};
